{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>And Esau despised the birthright.</b> \"He who despises a thing shall be held liable for it\" (Proverbs 13:13). But the reason for which he consented to the sale has already been stated: because he was likely to die by being hunted by wild beasts, and it is probable that he would die during his father's lifetime, and the birthright has no superiority except after the father — so what would the birthright profit him? And Scripture says, \"And he ate and he drank and he arose and he went and he despised\" — for after he ate and drank, he returned to the field to his hunting, and this is the cause of his despising the birthright: for the fools desire nothing but to eat and drink and do their pleasure at the moment, and they give no thought to the morrow.\n\nRabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] is greatly mistaken here, for he said that he despised the birthright because he saw that his father had no wealth. And many will wonder, for Abraham left him much wealth — as though they have never seen in their days a great rich man in his youth who comes to poverty in his old age. And the proof is that Isaac loved Esau because of his hunting; had there been abundant food in his father's house and he been honored in his own eyes, he would not have sold his birthright for a stew. And if his father ate delicacies every day, why would he say to him, \"Bring me game\"? And why did Jacob not have precious garments, and why did his mother not give him silver and gold for the journey, since he said (Genesis 28:20), \"And He will give me bread to eat and clothing to wear\"? Why did she not send him wealth when she loved him, so that he was forced to tend sheep? And the verse that says (Genesis 26:13), \"And the man grew great\" — that was before his old age. And the blind of heart think that wealth is a great virtue for the righteous — but Elijah will prove them wrong [n: i.e., Elijah who owned no wealth]. And they will further ask: why did HaShem deprive Isaac of wealth? Perhaps they can tell us why He deprived him of the light of his eyes. And let them not fob us off with a reed of derash, for it has a secret, and we must not search into it, for the thoughts of HaShem are deep and man has no power to understand them — all these are his [Ibn Ezra's] words.\n\nBut I marvel: who blinded the eyes of his intellect in this matter? For Abraham left him great wealth, and that wealth was lost before this episode, and because of this he despised the birthright. For this matter occurred in their youth, before Esau had wives, as Scripture recounts, and afterward he grew rich again in the land of the Philistines, until he became very great and the princes of the Philistines envied him, and afterward he returned to his poverty and craved his son's hunting and delicacies — and these are nothing but words of mockery.\n\nFurthermore, Scripture says (Genesis 25:11), \"And it came to pass after the death of Abraham, and Elohim blessed Isaac his son,\" and the blessing adds wealth and possessions and honor. Where was his blessing, that he lost his father's wealth and was impoverished, and afterward (Genesis 26:3), \"I will be with you and I will bless you\" — He made him rich and then impoverished him again? And if there are righteous men to whom befall the deeds of the wicked regarding wealth, this does not apply to those who were blessed from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He, for \"the blessing of YHWH, it makes rich, and He adds no sorrow with it\" (Proverbs 10:22).\n\nBut all the Patriarchs were like kings, and kings of the nations came before them and forged covenants with them, as it is written (Genesis 26:31), \"And they swore each man to his brother.\" And if Isaac had been ill-fated, losing his father's possessions, how could they say, \"We saw plainly that YHWH has been with you\" (v. 28), when he had already been among his adversaries [n: reading בְּעוֹכְרָיו as \"his adversaries/adversity\"; possibly corrupt]? Rather, Esau's despising of the birthright was because of the cruelty of his heart.\n\nIt is possible that the double portion in the birthright according to the law of the Torah was not so in former times, but rather it meant inheriting the father's preeminence and authority, so that he would have honor and superiority over his younger brother. Therefore he said to Isaac, \"I am your son, your firstborn\" (Genesis 27:32), to say that he is the firstborn fitting to be blessed, and likewise, \"For this is the firstborn; place your right hand upon his head\" (Genesis 48:18), to give him precedence in blessing. And perhaps he would also take slightly more in the inheritance, for the law of the double portion is an innovation of the Torah's law. And the game that was in his mouth — thus do princes and kings do, choosing game above all foods, and all the peoples bring from it tribute to the one they fear. And Esau would flatter his father by bringing all his game to his mouth to eat from it at his pleasure always, and the father's love for his firstborn son is easy to bring about. And what he said about blessing him after preparing delicacies for him is not payment for the food or a bribe therein, but he wished to benefit from him so that his soul would be bound to his soul at the time of enjoyment, and he would bless him with full desire and complete willingness. Or he knew in himself that after eating, his soul would be refreshed and joyful, and the spirit of holiness would alight upon it, as in the matter of \"And now bring me a musician, and it came to pass when the musician played, that the hand of YHWH was upon him\" (II Kings 3:15).\n\nThey did not put wealth in Jacob's hand because he was fleeing, and without his brother's knowledge he left the land alone; had they given him wealth and servants and camels, they would have increased Esau's envy to lie in wait for him and kill him. And our Rabbis said (Genesis Rabbah 68:2) that he was robbed of it. And who told him [Ibn Ezra] that Jacob did not have precious garments of fine linen and silk and embroidery? But Scripture says that when Esau went to the field to hunt, he would change into hunting garments, and because Isaac was always feeling his son and his garments, she dressed Jacob in them lest he recognize him by them. Do you not see that he did so: \"And he smelled the smell of his garments\" (Genesis 27:27) — for he would place them among nard and saffron, as in what is written (Psalms 45:9), \"Myrrh and aloes and cassia are all your garments.\" And the spices grew in the land of Israel, and therefore he said, \"like the smell of a field,\" for because he was a man of the field, his garments smelled of them, or the scent of the blossoms of the trees, as our Rabbis said (Ta'anit 29b): the smell of the field — of apple orchards.\n\nAnd the question about the light of his eyes is a question of the blind of heart, for if there was a cause from HaShem, it was in order that he might bless Jacob, and this is what Scripture recounts (Genesis 27:1): \"And his eyes grew dim from seeing, and he called Esau.\" And according to the plain sense, it is nothing but the matter of old age, and its reason is: \"And it came to pass when Isaac was old and his eyes grew dim in his old age, he called Esau.\" And behold, regarding Jacob (Genesis 48:10): \"And Israel's eyes were heavy from age; he could not see,\" and it is written of Ahijah the Shilonite (I Kings 14:4), \"For his eyes stood up from old age,\" and it tells a wonder about Moses our Teacher (Deuteronomy 34:7): \"His eye had not grown dim.\""], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The word בְּעוֹכְרָיו appears to mean \"his adversaries\" or \"his adversity\"; the text at this point may be slightly corrupt or elliptical."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:22:06.932672379Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}